   "fen": "r2qkb1r/pp3ppp/2n2n2/3p4/5Bb1/P1N1PN2/1PP2PPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "a3"
   ],
   "spoken": "The opponent wants to develop the bishop to b4 with a nasty pin that could wreck the queenside structure — so a rook-pawn to a3 pre-empts it. Delaying your own development is a real cost, but it's a judgement call worth making when the opponent is a pawn down and still uncoordinated.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 216.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp3ppp/1qn2n2/3p4/5Bb1/P1N1PN2/1PP2PPP/R2QKB1R w KQkq - 1 9",
   "line": [
    "Qb6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The queen sortie to b6 is less dangerous than it looks — it can simply be met by tucking the rook back to defend the pawn. Plain, working-person's play beats an ambitious blunder every time.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 241.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp3ppp/1qn2n2/3p4/5Bb1/P1N1PN2/1PP2PPP/1R1QKB1R b Kkq - 2 9",
